s="\"sbornik_csgt_2024.dvi (https://graphs.vsb.cz/csgt2024/files/abstracts.pdf)\\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; In 2016, Máčajová, Raspaud and Škoviera conjectured that all signed planar graphs are signed 4-colourable. Three years later, Kardoš and Narboni found a counterexample, after having translated the problem of signed 4-colourability of a planar triangulation into an equivalent problem in the dual – the existence of a so-called consistent semi-2-factor (i.e., a collection of disjoint cycles covering all positive vertices, with every cycle containing an even number of them) in a vertex signed dual 3-connected cubic planar graph.\\nTOWARDS THE SMALLEST SIGNED  \\nNON-4-COLOURABLE GRAPH\\n\\nFRANTIŠEK KARDOŠ, MATÚŠ MATOK*\\n\\nIn 2016, Máčajová, Raspaud and Škoviera conjectured that all signed planar graphs are signed 4-colourable. Three years later, Kardoš and Narboni found a counterexample, after having translated the problem of signed 4-colourability of a planar triangulation into an equivalent problem in the dual – the existence of a so-called consistent semi-2-factor (i.e., a collection of disjoint cycles covering all positive vertices, with every cycle containing an even number of them) in a vertex signed dual 3-connected cubic planar graph.\\n\\nWe study the properties of 3-edge-cuts and 4-edge-cuts with respect to consistent semi-2-factors in order to establish structural properties of a smallest counterexample. We classify 3-poles (graphs obtained by cutting along 3-edge-cuts) according to those properties into twelve classes, with the goal of searching exhaustively for a smallest representative of each class. We have developed a program that generates cubic planar graphs with specified structure (avoiding reducible configurations) and that verifies the existence of specific semi-2-factors. We have found the smallest representatives for six classes out of the twelve so far. The rest remains an open problem.\\n\\n24--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nAUSTRALASIAN JOURNAL OF COMBINATORICS (https://ajc.maths.uq.edu.au/pdf/80/ajc_v80_p419.pdf)\\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; not equitably 4-list arborable. ... For the sake of contradiction, suppose that f is an equitable, arborable\\nBy Proposition 2.4 we have |L−1({c1, c2}) ∩Y | ≥3,\\n|L−1({c1, c3}) ∩Y | ≥3, |L−1({d, c2}) ∩Y | ≥3, and |L−1({d, c3}) ∩Y | ≥3. This\\nimplies |Y | ≥12 which is a contradiction.\\nWe are ﬁnally ready to show that K11,17 is equitably 3-list arborable, but it is\\nnot equitably 4-list arborable.\\nTheorem 2.6. Let G = K11,17. Then, G is equitably 3-list arborable, but G is not\\nequitably 4-list arborable.\\nProof. Throughout the proof suppose the bipartition of G is X = {x1, . . . , x11},\\nY = {y1, . . . , y17}. First, we will show that G is not equitably 4-list arborable by\\nconstructing a 4-assignment L for G for which there is no equitable, arborable L-\\ncoloring of G. Suppose L is the 4-assignment for G that assigns the list {1, 2, 3, 4} to\\nevery vertex. For the sake of contradiction, suppose that f is an equitable, arborable\\nL-coloring of G.\\nWe know that |f −1(i)| ≤7 for each i ∈[4].\\nThis along with\\nthe fact that |V (G)| = 28 implies |f −1(i)| = 7 for each i ∈[4]. Without loss of\\ngenerality, suppose that |f −1(1) ∩X| ≥|f −1(2) ∩X| ≥|f −1(3) ∩X| ≥|f −1(4) ∩X|.\\nBy the fact that |X| = 11 and the Pigeonhole Principle, 2 ≤|f −1(2) ∩X| ≤5.\\nSo, |f −1(2) ∩Y | ≥2, and consequently f is not an arborable L-coloring of G by\\nProposition 2.1 which is a contradiction.\\nNow, we will show that G is equitably 3-list arborable. Suppose that L is an\\narbitrary 3-assignment for G. We will show that an arborable L-coloring of G that\\nuses no color more than ⌈|V (G)|/3⌉times exists. Since |V (G)| = 28 ≤23(4) −1,\\nwe know that an arborable L-coloring of G exists by Proposition 2.3. So, if each\\nc ∈\\u0002\\nv∈V (G) L(v) has the property that it appears in no more than 9 of the lists:\\nL(x1), . . . , L(x11) and no more than 9 of the lists: L(y1), . . . , L(y17), we are done\\nsince any arborable L-coloring of G will also be equitable if this holds.\\nSo, we just need to construct an equitable, arborable L-coloring of G in each of\\nthe following cases: (1) there is a c ∈\\u0002\\nv∈V (G) L(v) such that c appears in at least 10\\nof the lists: L(x1), . . . , L(x11) or (2) there is a c ∈\\u0002\\nv∈V (G) L(v) such that c appears\\nin at least 10 of the lists: L(y1), . . . , L(y17). In case (1) we can assume without loss\\nof generality that c ∈L(xi) for each i ∈[10]. Suppose we color each of x1, . . . , x10\\nwith c. Then, for each v ∈V (G) −{xi : i ∈[10]}, let L′(v) = L(v) −{c}. Now, we--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nAssume \\\\(\\\\Gamma\\\\) is a group with \\\\(|\\\\Gamma|=n\\\\) and \\\\(k\\\\) is a positive integer. Let \\\\(\\\\tau:\\\\Gamma\\\\to[n]\\\\) be a one-to-one correspondence from \\\\(\\\\Gamma\\\\) to \\\\([n]\\\\). Let \\\\(k'=kn+1\\\\) and for each \\\\(\\\\pi\\\\in\\\\Gamma\\\\), let \\\\(\\\\pi'\\\\) be the permutation of \\\\([k']\\\\) defined as (https://davinci.fmph.uniba.sk/~matok1/diplomovka/sources/Diplomovka.pdf)\\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.8 years ago; Further in his paper Zhu investigated \\\\(S\\\\)-4-colourability of planar graphs. ... That leaves just \\\\(S=\\\\{id,(12)(34)\\\\}\\\\), which was proven bad by Kardoš and Narboni when they disputed the conjecture of Máčajová, Raspaud and Škoviera that every signed-planar graph is 4-colourable. We will look into this conjecture and its counterexample in more detail. ... Máčajová, Raspaud and Škoviera defined the *chromatic number* \\\\(\\\\chi\\\\) of a signed graph \\\\((G,\\\\sigma)\\\\) as the least integer \\\\(k\\\\) such that \\\\((G,\\\\sigma)\\\\) is \\\\(k\\\\)-colorable, i.e., there exists a proper \\\\(k\\\\)-coloring of \\\\((G,\\\\sigma)\\\\) [6].\\n\\n**Definition 1.4** Assume \\\\(S\\\\) is a non-empty subset of \\\\(S_4\\\\). We say \\\\(S\\\\) is good if every planar graph is \\\\(S\\\\)-4-colourable, and \\\\(S\\\\) is bad otherwise.\\n\\nAs previously said, the four Colour Theorem is equivalent to stating that \\\\(S=\\\\{id\\\\}\\\\) is good. In this paper, they have proven that \\\\(S=\\\\{id,(123)\\\\}\\\\) and \\\\(S=\\\\{id,(1234)\\\\}\\\\) are bad. Král, Pangrác and Voss have proven that \\\\(S=\\\\{id,(1234),(13)(24)\\\\}\\\\) and \\\\(S=\\\\{id,(12)(34),(13)(24),(14)(23)\\\\}\\\\) are also bad. That leaves just \\\\(S=\\\\{id,(12)(34)\\\\}\\\\), which was proven bad by Kardoš and Narboni when they disputed the conjecture of Máčajová, Raspaud and Škoviera that every signed-planar graph is 4-colourable. We will look into this conjecture and its counterexample in more detail. As a result it was shown that subset \\\\(S\\\\) of \\\\(S_4\\\\) is good if and only if \\\\(S=\\\\{id\\\\}\\\\), which is a strengthening of the Four Colour Theorem.\\n\\nMáčajová, Raspaud and Škoviera defined the *chromatic number* \\\\(\\\\chi\\\\) of a signed graph \\\\((G,\\\\sigma)\\\\) as the least integer \\\\(k\\\\) such that \\\\((G,\\\\sigma)\\\\) is \\\\(k\\\\)-colorable, i.e., there exists a proper \\\\(k\\\\)-coloring of \\\\((G,\\\\sigma)\\\\) [6]. Furthermore, they have proven a few theorems about signed graphs in their paper.\\n\\n**Theorem 1.1** [6] For every loopless signed graph \\\\(G\\\\) we have\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\chi(G)\\\\leq 2\\\\chi(H)-1\\n\\\\]\\n\\nwhere \\\\(H\\\\) is the underlying graph of \\\\(G\\\\).\\n\\nFurthermore, this bound is sharp.\\n\\n**Theorem 1.2 (Brooks Theorem for signed planar graphs.)** [6] Let \\\\(G\\\\) be a simple connected signed graph. If \\\\(G\\\\) is not a balanced complete graph, a balanced odd circuit, or an unbalanced even circuit, then\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\chi(G)\\\\leq\\\\Delta(G).\\n\\\\]\\n\\n**Theorem 1.3** [6] For every simple signed planar graph \\\\(G\\\\) one has\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\chi_s(G)\\\\leq 5.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nFurthermore,--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nSeminár z teórie grafov - Matúš Matok (16.5.2024) (https://zona.fmph.uniba.sk/detail-novinky/browse/1/back_to_page/studium/article/seminar-z-teorie-grafov-matus-matok-1652024/calendar_date/2024/may/)\\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Crawled: last month; Názov: Towards the smallest signed planar non-4-colourable graph ... In 2019, Kardoš and Narboni constructed a counterexample to the 4-colour theorem for signed planar graphs, proposed as a conjecture by Máčajová, Raspaud and Škoviera in 2016.\\n\\n# Seminár z teórie grafov - Matúš Matok (16.5.2024)\\n\\n## vo štvrtok 16.4.2024 o 9:50 hod. v posluchárni C\\n\\n* * *\\n\\n13. 05. 2024 15.47 hod.\\nOd: Martin Škoviera\\n\\nPrednášajúci: Matúš Matok\\n\\nNázov: Towards the smallest signed planar non-4-colourable graph\\n\\nTermín: 16.5.2024, 9:50 hod., poslucháreň C\\n\\nAbstrakt:\\nIn 2019, Kardoš and Narboni constructed a counterexample to the 4-colour theorem for signed planar graphs, proposed as a conjecture by Máčajová, Raspaud and Škoviera in 2016. This counterexample consisted of gadgets with significant properties. Gadgets, in the form of tripoles, became the area of our interest. Therefore, we introduced a classification of tripoles and set out to find the smallest representative of each class. In this talk, we will discuss the details of the exhaustive search for these representatives.\\n\\nStránka seminára--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nOn Equitable List Arboricity of Graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.08926)\\nciteturn1academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.2 years ago; Graph $G$ is equitably $k$-list arborable if an equitable, arborable list coloring of $G$ exists for every list assignment for $G$ that associates with each vertex in $G$ a list of $k$ available colors. ... We also show that if $G$ is equitably $k$-list arborable it does not necessarily follow that $G$ is equitably $(k+1)$-list arborable which addresses a question of Drgas-Burchardt, Furmanczyk, and Sidorowicz (2018).\\nTitle: On Equitable List Arboricity of Graphs\\nAuthors: Hemanshu Kaul, Jeffrey A. Mudrock, Michael J. Pelsmajer\\nDate: Wed Aug 19 14:56:33 2020\\n\\nEquitable list arboricity, introduced by Zhang in 2016, generalizes the notion of equitable list coloring by requiring the subgraph induced by each color class to be acyclic (instead of edgeless) in addition to the usual upper bound on the size of each color class. Graph $G$ is equitably $k$-list arborable if an equitable, arborable list coloring of $G$ exists for every list assignment for $G$ that associates with each vertex in $G$ a list of $k$ available colors. Zhang conjectured that any graph $G$ is equitably $k$-list arborable for each $k$ satisfying $k \\\\geq \\\\lceil (1+\\\\Delta(G))/2 \\\\rceil$. We verify this conjecture for powers of cycles by applying a new lemma which is a general tool for extending partial equitable, arborable list colorings. We also propose a stronger version of Zhang's Conjecture for certain connected graphs: any connected graph $G$ is equitably $k$-list arborable for each $k$ satisfying $k \\\\geq \\\\lceil \\\\Delta(G)/2 \\\\rceil$ provided $G$ is neither a cycle nor a complete graph of odd order. We verify this stronger version of Zhang's Conjecture for powers of paths, 2-degenerate graphs, and certain other graphs. We also show that if $G$ is equitably $k$-list arborable it does not necessarily follow that $G$ is equitably $(k+1)$-list arborable which addresses a question of Drgas-Burchardt, Furmanczyk, and Sidorowicz (2018).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nList colourings of planar graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/295512654_List_colourings_of_planar_graphs)\\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; A graph G = G (V, E) is called L-list colourable if there is a vertex colouring of G in which the colour assigned to a vertex v is chosen from a list L (v) associated with this vertex. ... The list-coloring counterpart of the conjecture is also false: every counterexample to 4-choosability of an unsigned planar graph (e.g. Voigt [45]) gives, via the all-positive signature, a signed planar graph that is not signed 4-choosable. ... From the strengthened extension statement (\\\\cref{thm:main}) we deduce the main result (\\\\cref{thm:JKS}: \\\\chs\\\\Gs\\\\le 5 for every planar signed graph), the Máčajová--Raspaud--Škoviera signed Five-Color Theorem in the symmetric palette \\\\Ns{2}=\\\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\\\}, the Switching Invariance Lemma, 3-choosability of outerplanar signed graphs, 1-defective signed 4-choosability of planar signed graphs, a sandwich inequality relating \\\\chs to the unsigned and positive'' choice numbers, and a polynomial-time list-coloring algorithm.\\n\\n## No full-text available\\n\\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\\n\\nTo read the full-text of this research,\\nyou can request a copy directly from the author.\\n\\nRequest full-text PDF\\n\\n## Citations (180)\\n\\n## References (5)\\n\\n... We can now state the principal results. Voigt's planar non-4-choosable graph G 0 [45], viewed as (G 0 , +1), satisfies χ s ℓ (G 0 , +1) ≥ 5, so the signed list chromatic number can reach 5 already on planar signed graphs (see also Mirzakhani [29]). The complementary upper bound was established by Jin, Kang, and Steffen. ...\\n\\n... Example 6.1 (Voigt's planar graph as a signed graph). Voigt [45] constructed a planar graph G 0 on 238 vertices that is not 4-choosable, with explicit list assignment L of size 4 at every vertex. Endow G 0 with the all-positive signature σ 0 ≡ +1. ...\\n\\n... The list-coloring counterpart of the conjecture is also false: every counterexample to 4-choosability of an unsigned planar graph (e.g. Voigt [45]) gives, via the all-positive signature, a signed planar graph that is not signed 4-choosable. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n$4$-Colouring of Generalized Signed Planar Graphs (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/view/v27i3p31)\\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.2 years ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; # $4$-Colouring of Generalized Signed Planar Graphs ... This paper studies the problem as to which subsets $S$ of $S_4$, every  planar graph is $S$-$4$-colourable.\\n\\n# $4$-Colouring of Generalized Signed Planar Graphs\\n\\n  * Yiting Jiang\\n  * Xuding Zhu\\n\\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.37236/9338\\n\\n### Abstract\\n\\nAssume $G$ is a graph and $S$ is a set of permutations of positive integers. An $S$-signature of $G$ is a pair $(D, \\\\sigma)$, where $D$ is an orientation of $G$ and $\\\\sigma: E(D) \\\\to S$ is a mapping which assigns to each arc $e=(u,v)$ a permutation $\\\\sigma(e)$ in $S$. We say $G$ is $S$-$k$-colourable if for any $S$-signature $(D, \\\\sigma)$ of $G$, there is a mapping $f: V(G) \\\\to [k]$ such that for each arc $e=(u,v)$ of $G$, $\\\\sigma(e)(f(u)) \\\\ne f(v)$. The concept of $S$-$k$-colourable is a common generalization of many colouring concepts. This paper studies the problem as to which subsets $S$ of $S_4$, every  planar graph is $S$-$4$-colourable. We call such a subset $S$ of $S_4$ a good subset. The Four Colour Theorem is equivalent to saying that $S=\\\\{id\\\\}$ is good. It was proved by Jin, Wong and Zhu (arXiv:1811.08584) that a subset $S$ containing $id$ is good if and only if $S=\\\\{id\\\\}$. In this paper, we prove that, up to conjugation, every good subset of $S_4$ not containing $id$ is a subset of $\\\\{(12),(34),(12)(34)\\\\}$.\\n\\nPublished\\n\\n2020-08-21\\n\\nHow to Cite\\n\\nJiang, Y., & Zhu, X. (2020). $4$-Colouring of Generalized Signed Planar Graphs. The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, 27(3), #P3.31. https://doi.org/10.37236/9338\\n\\nIssue\\n\\nVolume 27, Issue 3 (2020)\\n\\nArticle Number\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nSigned planar graphs with given circular chromatic numbers (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0012365X22002266)\\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Disproving a conjecture of Máčajová, Raspaud and Škoviera, Kardoš and Narboni constructed a non-4-colourable simple signed planar graph, or equivalently, a simple signed planar graph with circular chromatic number greater than 4. ... A signed graph is a graph G together with a signature σ which assigns to each edge e a sign . ... It is natural to ask if the Four Colour Theorem can be extended to simple signed planar graphs. ... In this paper, we further strengthen this result and prove the following theorem.\\n\\n## Discrete Mathematics\\n\\nDate: November 2022\\n\\nArticle: 113020\\n\\nVolume: Volume 345, Issue 11\\n\\n# Signed planar graphs with given circular chromatic numbers\\n\\nAuthor links open overlay panel Yangyan Gu, Xuding Zhu ^{1}\\n\\nShow more\\n\\nCite\\n\\nAdd to Mendeley\\n\\nShare\\n\\n10.1016/j.disc.2022.113020\\n\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nDisproving a conjecture of Máčajová, Raspaud and Škoviera, Kardoš and Narboni constructed a non-4-colourable simple signed planar graph, or equivalently, a simple signed planar graph with circular chromatic number greater than 4. Naserasr, Wang, and Zhu improved this result by constructing a simple signed planar graph with circular chromatic number 14/3. This paper further strengthens this result by constructing, for each rational , a simple signed planar graph with circular chromatic number . Together with some earlier results of Moser and Zhu, this implies that every rational is the circular chromatic number of a simple signed planar graph.\\n\\n## Keywords\\n\\nSigned planar graph\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nColouring of S -labelled planar graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/343796839_Colouring_of_S_-labelled_planar_graphs)\\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; The later result combined with the above result of Kemnitz and Voigt disproves a conjecture of Kang and Steffen that every planar graph is signed Z4-colourable. We shall show that a graph constructed by Wegner in 1973 is also a counterexample to Kang and Steffen's conjecture, and present a new construction of a non-{1,3}-choosable planar graphs.\\nA very recent result of Kemnitz and Voigt implies that for any partition λ of 4 other than {1,1,1,1}, there is a planar graph which is not λ-choosable. We observe that, in contrast to the fact that there are non-4-choosable 3-chromatic planar graphs, every 3-chromatic planar graph is {1,3}-choosable, and that if G is a planar graph whose dual G⁎ has a connected spanning Eulerian subgraph, then G is {2,2}-choosable. We prove that if n is a positive even integer, λ is a partition of n−1 in which each part is at most 3, then Kn is edge λ-choosable. Finally we study relations between λ-choosability of graphs and colouring of signed graphs and generalized signed graphs. A conjecture of Máčajová, Raspaud and Škoviera that every planar graph is signed 4-colcourable is recently disproved by Kardoš and Narboni. We prove that every signed 4-colourable graph is weakly 4-choosable, and every signed Z4-colourable graph is {1,1,2}-choosable. The later result combined with the above result of Kemnitz and Voigt disproves a conjecture of Kang and Steffen that every planar graph is signed Z4-colourable. We shall show that a graph constructed by Wegner in 1973 is also a counterexample to Kang and Steffen's conjecture, and present a new construction of a non-{1,3}-choosable planar graphs.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nThe Alon-Tarsi number of a planar graph minus a matching (https://doi.org/10.1016%2Fj.jctb.2020.02.005)\\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; In the proof of Theorem 6, in Case 2, instead of adding the edge 𝑢𝑖⁢𝑣𝑛 to the matching M, we may increase the power of 𝑢𝑖 by 1. ... A signed graph is a pair (𝐺,𝜎), where G is a graph and σ is a signature of G which assigns to each edge e of G a sign 𝜎𝑒 ∈{1,−1}. ... If (𝐺,𝜎) is a signed planar graph, then G has a matching M such that 𝐴⁢𝑇⁡(𝐺− 𝑀,𝜎) ≤4. ... Every signed planar graph (𝐺,𝜎) has a subset X of vertices with |𝑋|< |𝑉⁡(𝐺)|/2 such that if L is a list assignment which assigns to each vertex in X five permissible colours and assigns to each other vertex four permissible colours, then (𝐺,𝜎) is L-colourable.\\nThe chromatic number 𝜒⁡(𝐺,𝜎) of (𝐺,𝜎) is the minimum integer k such that for any set S of k integers, (𝐺,𝜎) has a proper colouring using colours from S. The choice number of (𝐺,𝜎) is the minimum integer k such that for every k-list assignment L of G, there is a proper L-colouring of (𝐺,𝜎). The polynomial associated to (𝐺,𝜎) is defined as 𝑃𝐺,𝜎⁡(𝚡)=∏𝑢∼𝑣,𝑢<𝑣(𝑣−𝜎⁡(𝑢⁢𝑣)⁢𝑢). The Alon-Tarsi number of signed graphs is defined similarly. Then all the arguments in the proof of Theorem 6 work. Hence we have the following result.\\n\\nTheorem 9\\n\\nIf (𝐺,𝜎) is a signed planar graph, then G has a matching M such that 𝐴⁢𝑇⁡(𝐺− 𝑀,𝜎) ≤4. Consequently, (𝐺− 𝑀,𝜎) is 4-choosable, and (𝐺,𝜎) itself is 1-defective 4-choosable.\\n\\nCorollary 8 also works for signed planar graphs.\\n\\nCorollary 10\\n\\nEvery signed planar graph (𝐺,𝜎) has a subset X of vertices with |𝑋|< |𝑉⁡(𝐺)|/2 such that if L is a list assignment which assigns to each vertex in X five permissible colours and assigns to each other vertex four permissible colours, then (𝐺,𝜎) is L-colourable.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nColouring of generalized signed planar graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/329116125_Colouring_of_generalized_signed_planar_